sections.body:
try/catch catches a **synchronous** throw that happens within the dynamic extent of the try block: the call stack that includes the try block has to still be executing when the throw happens, so that unwinding it can reach the matching catch. That requirement breaks as soon as an operation becomes asynchronous:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

try {
  fs.readFile('./does-not-exist.txt', (err, data) => {
    if (err) throw err;   // throwing here does NOT get caught by the try/catch below
    console.log(data);
  });
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Caught it:', err.message);   // never runs
}
```

By the time fs.readFile's callback executes, after the file system operation has completed and been dispatched back through the event loop, the original try block has finished running (fs.readFile returned immediately, since it's non-blocking) and the stack frame containing that try/catch is gone. Throwing inside the callback throws into whatever calls the callback at that later point, deep inside Node's internals, where no matching catch exists. The result is an uncaught exception (covered in the next concept), not a caught one. This is why the async patterns concept stressed checking the err argument (if (err) { ... }) instead of throwing: it isn't a style preference but the only mechanism available, because try/catch can't bridge a gap in time.

Since a throw inside an async callback has nowhere useful to land, Node's core APIs never do that for expected failure conditions. They follow the error-first convention instead, passing the error object as the callback's first argument, to the one piece of code guaranteed to run when the operation completes, successfully or not. This turns 'catching an error' into 'reading a value,' which works across any gap in time because it's an ordinary function argument rather than something that depends on a live call stack:

```javascript
fs.readFile('./does-not-exist.txt', (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error('Failed to read file:', err.message);   // this DOES run
    return;
  }
  console.log(data);
});
```

The consequence is that error-first callback code needs its error handling written by hand at every callback boundary. No ambient try/catch does it for you, and skipping the if (err) check isn't a syntax error, just a path straight into using data that might be undefined.

Promises solve the same problem, delivering a failure that happens later across an asynchronous gap, with a different mechanism. Instead of an argument you have to remember to check, a rejected Promise skips every subsequent .then() handler in its chain until it reaches a .catch() (or a second argument to .then), the way a synchronous throw skips code until it reaches a matching catch block:

```javascript
fetchUser(id)
  .then((user) => fetchOrders(user.id))
  .then((orders) => {
    if (orders.length === 0) {
      throw new Error('No orders found');   // this throw becomes a REJECTION of the chain
    }
    return summarize(orders);
  })
  .then((summary) => console.log(summary))
  .catch((err) => console.error('Something failed:', err.message));
```

Notice the throw new Error(...) inside the second .then() handler. Throwing synchronously inside a .then() callback is converted into a rejection of the Promise that .then() call returns, which propagates onward like a rejection from fetchUser or fetchOrders would; .then() does the conversion for you. A single .catch() at the end of the chain is reached whichever step failed, which is the advantage over callback style: no repeated if (err) check at every link. When you catch an error only to rethrow a more descriptive one, pass the original along with the `cause` option, as in throw new Error('Could not load orders', { cause: err }), so the underlying failure and its stack aren't lost; err.cause is available to whatever catches it later.

await unwraps a Promise's eventual outcome back into synchronous-looking control flow: a resolved value becomes the expression's result, and a rejection is re-thrown at that line, which makes try/catch usable again inside an async function. A finally block likewise runs after the awaited work settles, whether it succeeded or threw, so it's the right place to release a connection or stop a timer. The basic shape:

```javascript
async function getOrderSummary(id) {
  try {
    const user = await fetchUser(id);
    const orders = await fetchOrders(user.id);
    if (orders.length === 0) throw new Error('No orders found');
    return summarize(orders);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Something failed:', err.message);
    return null;
  }
}
```

The important word is *await*: try/catch only catches a rejection from a Promise you awaited (or otherwise attached a handler to) within that try block. A very common mistake is forgetting the await on a Promise-returning call inside a try block that otherwise looks correct:

```javascript
async function getOrderSummaryBuggy(id) {
  try {
    const user = await fetchUser(id);
    const orders = fetchOrders(user.id);   // BUG: missing await
    return summarize(orders);              // summarize gets a Promise, not the real orders
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Something failed:', err.message);   // never runs if fetchOrders rejects
    return null;
  }
}
```

Because fetchOrders(user.id) is never awaited, its Promise is handed to summarize, which probably breaks. More importantly, if fetchOrders *rejects* later, that happens outside the try block's awareness: the try/catch has moved past that line, since nothing paused for it. The rejection becomes unhandled, the scenario the next section and the next concept deal with.

Every mechanism above assumes something downstream is paying attention: awaiting the Promise, or attaching a .catch() to it. That assumption breaks when an async function is handed to code that has no concept of Promises, which describes most event-emitter-style APIs:

```javascript
const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  const data = await fetchDataThatMightReject();   // if this rejects...
  res.end(JSON.stringify(data));
});
// http.createServer's internals call this handler and do NOT await its returned
// Promise or attach a .catch() to it, so a rejection here becomes unhandled
```

http.createServer (and EventEmitter.on more generally) invokes the handler you give it and moves on. It doesn't know the function is async or that it returns a Promise, and it never awaits or catches anything from it. If fetchDataThatMightReject() rejects, the rejection has nowhere to go: there's no try/catch inside the handler, and no caller awaits the outer async function's returned Promise, since http.createServer never looks at it. The result is an unhandled promise rejection. For that request, the client's connection hangs (res.end() is never called), and on Node 15 and later the process itself crashes by default. The fix is always the same shape: wrap the body of any async function handed to a Promise-unaware API in its own try/catch, so failures are caught locally instead of relying on a caller that was never designed to notice them.

examples.code:
const fs = require('fs');

function loadConfigBroken() {
  try {
    fs.readFile('./config.json', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
      if (err) throw err;   // throws into fs.readFile's internal completion machinery, not here
      return JSON.parse(data);
    });
  } catch (err) {
    console.log('This never runs for a missing file, even though config.json is missing');
  }
  console.log('loadConfigBroken() returned, before the file read has even finished');
}

loadConfigBroken();

function fetchUser(id) {
  return id > 0
    ? Promise.resolve({ id, name: 'Amy' })
    : Promise.reject(new Error('Invalid user id'));
}

function fetchOrders(userId) {
  return Promise.resolve([]);   // simulate: this user has no orders
}

fetchUser(-1)
  .then((user) => fetchOrders(user.id))
  .then((orders) => {
    if (orders.length === 0) throw new Error('No orders found');
    return orders.length;
  })
  .then((count) => console.log(`Order count: ${count}`))
  .catch((err) => console.error('Chain failed:', err.message));
// logs: 'Chain failed: Invalid user id' (fetchOrders and the length check never run)

async function processOrder(orderId) {
  try {
    console.log('Starting order processing...');
    chargeCard(orderId);   // BUG: missing await, so this is a Promise, not a result
    console.log('Charge succeeded, shipping order.');   // runs regardless of the real outcome
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Order failed:', err.message);   // never runs, even if chargeCard rejects
  }
}

function chargeCard(orderId) {
  return Promise.reject(new Error('Card declined'));
}

processOrder(101);
// Logs 'Starting order processing...' then 'Charge succeeded, shipping order.'
// ...even though the card was declined, and then an unhandled rejection crashes the process

