sections.body:
process.on('uncaughtException', handler) fires when a synchronous throw (or an error thrown inside a callback, as in the previous concept) is never caught by any try/catch in the call stack it propagates through. process.on('unhandledRejection', handler) fires when a Promise rejects and nothing (no .catch(), no awaiting try/catch) handles the rejection; since Node 15, an unhandled rejection crashes the process by default, as an uncaught exception always has. In fact, with the default setting an unhandled rejection is raised as an uncaught exception, so an uncaughtException handler also receives it, and its second argument, origin, tells you which it was ('uncaughtException' or 'unhandledRejection').

These events can look like an invitation to write a handler that logs the error and keeps the process running, neutralizing every crash. Node's documentation says otherwise: **'the correct use of uncaughtException is to perform synchronous cleanup of allocated resources ... it is not safe to resume normal operation after uncaughtException.'** This follows from the previous section: an uncaught exception escaped every try/catch in the code, which almost always means a programmer error nobody anticipated, the category where continuing risks operating on corrupted state. The legitimate use is narrow: log the error with enough detail to diagnose it later, flush pending writes or close connections if you can do so quickly and synchronously, and then call process.exit(1), not swallow the error and pretend nothing happened.

```javascript
process.on('uncaughtException', (err) => {
  console.error('Fatal error, shutting down:', err);
  // synchronous cleanup only: do not attempt further async work here
  process.exit(1);
});
```

JavaScript's Error type doesn't track whether an error is operational or a programmer error, so a common pattern is a custom error class that carries a flag:

```javascript
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode = 500) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'AppError';
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
    this.isOperational = true;   // marks this as an anticipated failure
  }
}

// Usage in a route:
app.get('/users/:id', async (req, res, next) => {
  const user = await db.findUser(req.params.id);
  if (!user) {
    return next(new AppError('User not found', 404));   // operational: expected, handled
  }
  res.json(user);
});

// Error-handling middleware (recognized by its 4-parameter signature, from the building_with_node module)
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  if (err.isOperational) {
    res.status(err.statusCode).json({ error: err.message });
  } else {
    console.error('Unexpected error, crashing for a clean restart:', err);
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
    process.exit(1);   // let a process manager restart with a known-good state (real code would close the server first so the response isn't cut off)
  }
});
```

The .isOperational flag turns an implicit, easy-to-get-wrong judgment call ('is this an error I should recover from, or one that means I no longer trust my own state?') into a decision made where the error is created. A missing user is created as an AppError with isOperational: true because the code that created it knew this was an anticipated case; any unmarked error reaching the same middleware is treated as a sign that something unexpected happened. One caveat on the route above: in Express 4, a rejection from await db.findUser(...) is not forwarded to next() automatically, so the handler would need its own try/catch calling next(err), as in the exercise below; Express 5 forwards rejected promises from async handlers to the error middleware.

examples.code:
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message);
    this.isOperational = true;
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
  }
}

async function getDiscountForUser(userId) {
  const user = await db.findUser(userId);
  if (!user) {
    // Anticipated: a caller might pass an id that doesn't exist, which is not a bug
    throw new AppError('User not found', 404);
  }
  // If db.findUser's return shape ever changes and `user.tier` doesn't
  // exist, THIS throws a plain, unmarked TypeError: a programmer error, not
  // something this function anticipated or is trying to represent on purpose
  return discountRatesByTier[user.tier.toUpperCase()];
}

const server = http.createServer(app);
server.listen(3000);

process.on('uncaughtException', (err) => {
  console.error('Uncaught exception, shutting down:', err);
  // Stop accepting NEW connections, but let in-flight ones finish briefly
  server.close(() => {
    process.exit(1);
  });
  // Safety net: force-exit if close() takes too long (e.g. a stuck connection)
  setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 5000).unref();
});

// DANGEROUS: treats every uncaught exception as recoverable
process.on('uncaughtException', (err) => {
  console.error('Error occurred, but continuing:', err.message);
  // no process.exit(), so the process keeps running
});

let cache = {};

function updateCache(key, value) {
  cache[key].push(value);   // BUG: assumes cache[key] already exists as an array
}

updateCache('scores', 10);   // throws TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined
// With the handler above, this error is logged and the process keeps running,
// but `cache` may now be missing an update it dropped, and every future
// caller of updateCache has no idea whether this call succeeded

