sections.body:
Rather than clicking through a UI to set a breakpoint every time you restart your debugging session, you can write the spot you want to pause at into the code:

```javascript
function calculateDiscount(order) {
  const baseDiscount = order.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.discount, 0);
  debugger;   // execution pauses here if a debugger client is attached
  return Math.min(baseDiscount, order.total * 0.5);
}
```

The debugger statement is part of the JavaScript language (not Node-specific), and its behavior is conditional. If a debugging client is attached when execution reaches that line, it pauses as if you'd set a breakpoint there; if no debugger is attached (a normal node app.js run, with no --inspect flag), it's a no-op, no different from a comment. That makes it reasonably safe to leave in temporarily while debugging (remove it before committing; a stray debugger statement is a common code review nitpick), and you don't have to navigate a UI to the right line every time you restart a session: you attach, and it's waiting where you left it.

examples.code:
// app.js
function getDiscountedPrice(order) {
  debugger;   // pause here once a debugger client attaches
  return order.price * (1 - order.discountRate);
}

const order = { price: 100 };   // BUG: discountRate is missing from this object
console.log(getDiscountedPrice(order));   // NaN: 1 - undefined is NaN, with no error

// Run with: node --inspect-brk app.js
// Then attach via chrome://inspect (or VS Code). Execution pauses at the
// debugger statement, so you can inspect `order` and see immediately that
// discountRate is missing, rather than working backward from the NaN result.

const results = [
  { endpoint: '/users', status: 200, ms: 45 },
  { endpoint: '/orders', status: 500, ms: 812 },
  { endpoint: '/health', status: 200, ms: 3 },
];

console.table(results);

console.time('process-results');
const slowOnes = results.filter((r) => r.ms > 100);
console.timeEnd('process-results');   // 'process-results: 0.412ms' (or similar)

console.warn(`${slowOnes.length} slow endpoint(s) detected`);

console.log('Server started on port 3000');       // goes to stdout
console.error('Failed to connect to Redis cache');  // goes to stderr

// Running: node app.js > output.txt
// 'Server started on port 3000' is written into output.txt.
// 'Failed to connect to Redis cache' still prints to the terminal,
// since only stdout was redirected, not stderr.

